<!-- Website-only teaching for android/observability/logging.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">A release logger ignores a debug message, but the expensive expression inside that message still runs. Logging has two boundaries: constructing the argument and deciding what reaches a sink.</p>

Follow both boundaries, then decide what diagnostic value justifies CPU, privacy and retention cost.

<!-- @after Internal Mechanics :: visual -->

### Policy starts after ordinary arguments are evaluated

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow" style="--cols:3"><div class="node"><strong>Call-site work</strong><small>Interpolation and function arguments can run before the facade is called.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Logging policy</strong><small>Configured trees or wrappers select, sample and redact.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Sink</strong><small>Logcat or remote diagnostics retain only approved output.</small></div></div><figcaption>Runtime suppression alone does not prove argument work disappeared. Verify release transformations and use a genuinely lazy boundary when needed.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Failure Modes :: animation -->

### Why a silent logger can still do work

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">Why a silent logger can still do work</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="call"><strong>Call site</strong><small>Build debug message</small></div><div class="node" data-el="heavy"><strong>Argument</strong><small>Expensive calculation</small></div><div class="node" data-el="tree"><strong>Runtime tree</strong><small>Reject debug output</small></div><div class="node" data-el="sink"><strong>Sink</strong><small>No message written</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Evaluate", "caption": "In this illustrative ordinary eager call, the message expression calls the expensive calculation before entering the logging facade.", "state": {"call": "active", "heavy": "active"}, "add": ["call", "heavy", "tree", "sink"]}, {"label": "Filter", "caption": "The runtime policy rejects the debug message. That decision cannot undo work already performed.", "state": {"heavy": "done", "tree": "active"}}, {"label": "Suppress", "caption": "No message reaches the sink. Silence is evidence about output, not about the earlier argument cost.", "state": {"tree": "done", "sink": "ghost"}}, {"label": "Verify the boundary", "caption": "Use an appropriate guard or lazy wrapper for material argument work and inspect the optimised release behavior. Do not assume a facade guarantees removal.", "state": {"call": "done", "heavy": "warn"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->

Keep the diagnostic schema small and owned. Test sink routing, redaction and runtime cost separately; one successful filter does not validate the other boundaries.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>Can a release tree stop an already-evaluated argument?</summary>

No. Put the policy before material evaluation or use a lazy boundary; verify the actual release artifact.

</details>

<details><summary>Does an error priority make a message safe to upload?</summary>

No. Classify and minimise its content before any sink receives it.

</details>

<details><summary>What proves a logging optimisation worked?</summary>

Release-artifact/runtime evidence on the relevant call path, including argument work and retained output.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

Primary evidence for the added layer: [Timber API and routing](https://github.com/JakeWharton/timber), [Android Log](https://developer.android.com/reference/android/util/Log).
